Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:07.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  i'm sure you've seen this image before  it's the great wave by hokusai it's  perhaps the most recognizable piece of  japanese art from japan's most famous  artist but around the same time as  making this print hokusai was also  conjuring up strange and frightening  ghosts plucked from japanese folklore  from the story of oiwa a possessed  lantern who haunts her treacherous  husband to the kahadu kaheji a vengeful  corpse that peers over the curtain to  watch you sleep but where did these  monsters come from and why was hokusai  so fixated with ghost stories all this  and more as we step into the ghoulish  art of supernatural japan  our tale begins in japan's edo period  which spanned between the years of 1603  to 1867 a time when the country's rulers  restricted contact with the outside  world with such limited foreign  influence art within japan's borders  flourished particularly a style of wood  block printing called ukiyoe images were  carved onto a piece of wood then covered  in ink and then finally pressed onto  paper revealing the image this method of  printing produced some of the most  amazing artists and artworks but  arguably the master of a kiwi was this  man katsushika hokzai it's believed that  hogwarts created 30 000 unique pieces of  art including his famous hogwarts manga  a 13 volume collection of sketches on  every topic imaginable people animals  plants mythology architecture even  self-defense techniques he also made  some stunning landscapes  can we take a minute to appreciate some  of them  by the age of 70 hokketsai was known  throughout japan but he's yet to have  created his most famous work  his 36 views of mount fuji one of them  included his masterpiece under the great  wave of kanagawa here hawkside depicts a  rogue wave as it plunges down onto the  boats below  this fearsome and dramatic piece was  instantly popular in japan in his  lifetime some five thousand copies were  printed but only a handful of these  originals have survived as he grew into  his old age hakuzai became more and more  interested in the supernatural he spent  much of his time making frightening  prints of ghosts from folklore or as  they're referred to in japan yokai  yokai are weird and mysterious beings  eerie apparitions that inhabit  supernatural japan there's no word in  english that accurately conveys the  meaning of youkai in japanese the term  refers to things that are both strange  and beautiful i can see why they come in  all shapes and sizes from friendly  household spirits to fierce demons let  me introduce you to some of them now up  first there's the oyajirome with its  large bulbous eye on the back of its  head or the domo komo a spirit with two  heads and two personalities what about  the fearsome ushioni one part bull one  part crab and the rest nightmare fuel  but my favorite is the kamakiri this  cheeky bird goblin that goes around  cutting long hair  now around the time that hokusai was  active interest in the supernatural  yokai had boomed trading ghost stories  was a favorite pastime particularly in  the game of hyakumonogatari where  participants would sit in a candlelit  room and take turns telling frightening  tales after each one was shared a candle  would be extinguished until there was no  light left in the room  it was there that the yokai would appear  as an aide to tell these stories  woodblock artists were often  commissioned to provide illustrations  this included hokusei who made a print  series called 100 ghost stories each of  his five images have their own legend to  accompany them and i want to tell you  this one it's the tale of oiwa and it's  one of the most popular ghost stories in  japan  oiwa lived in the city of edo with her  husband iamon yemon was a samurai a  noble warrior of old except that there  was nothing noble about this samurai for  he was secretly having an affair with  their neighbour one day oiwa felt very  sick each day she would get worse and  worse  seeing an opportunity to finally rid  himself of his sickly wife eemon gave  oiwa a potion believing it to be  medicine oiwa drank the concoction  however it was a deadly poison and soon  she was beset by horrific changes her  hair fell out and she was driven mad in  the grips of her illness she fell onto  her husband's sword her spirit passed  into a nearby lantern and she was  condemned to roam the earth as a  vengeful specter day and night she would  haunt emmaud for his betrayal eventually  driving him to madness as well  this super depressing story is captured  in one single image and looking closely  it contains some prompts to help the  storyteller over here we have oiwa's  name and this symbol on our forehead is  the mark of the buddhist god yama the  evil lord of death and the underworld  the other ghost stories are equally as  disturbing with many of them being about  revenge this eerie wood block tells the  story of the servant girl sarashiki  whose spirit emerges out of a well each  night in search of an expensive plate  she once lost hence the snake-like body  made out of crockery  we've also got kahada koiji a former  kabuki actor who was murdered and then  dumped in a swamp in this picture he's  pulling apart the mosquito net having  just found his murderers of course  hoksai wasn't the only artist who  enjoyed painting yokai contemporaries of  hakusai like kunioshi made their own  versions like the story of umibozu a  giant ghostly shadow who appears to  sailors in fierce typhoons but beware  one wrong word and you'll be dragged to  the depths of the sea  these illustrations of yokai showcase  the bizarre imaginations of woodblock  artists similar creatures appear in  european artworks like these headless  humanoids called blemis if you want to  learn more about them consider watching  my field guide to medieval monsters  in the years after hockeytime's death  japan opened its borders to the outside  world hockside's exquisite woodblock  prince reached a wider audience the  great wave in particular today it's one  of the most widely printed artworks of  all time the introduction of western art  led to other changes traditional  japanese manga pioneered by hokusai  morphed with american comic books to  create the manga we know today  even in animation western styles fused  with traditional tastes and practices as  seen in this early experimental cartoon  this evolved into anime and it's through  these mediums we've got to experience  all the wonderful yokai that have come  out of japan a prime example of this is  the animation of studio ghibli in films  like spirited away or pompoco both are  love letters to japanese folklore and  artists like hakusai you might be able  to tell by now but hokusai is one of my  favorite artists and whilst his  woodblock landscapes get most attention  there's something so wonderful about his  ghostly prince there's nothing quite  like them they're so animated and  strange through his spectral yokai  hokusai hints at wonders and horrors  that lurk behind our everyday lives  [Music]  hey thanks for watching i really hope  you enjoyed this one japanese folklore  is certainly something i'm going to  return to so stick around for more if  you like strange and disturbing art then  you'll love my video on the nightmarish  hellscapes of euronymous bosch if you  liked this video and want to see more  why not subscribe a like and a comment  also go a really long way  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time  goodbye  [Music]  you

